\newlabel{tab:three_way}{{1}{10}{In-sample IV RMSE on 234{,}549 observations, reported in volatility percentage points. Each model fitted one ticker level jointly with its shape parameters. The final row used per-ticker neural surfaces for the 29 qualified tickers and sector surfaces for PFE and BMY}{table.caption.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces In-sample comparison of the implied volatility (IV) observed on May 11, 2026 with surfaces fitted across all fifteen calibration snapshots. Panels show (a) SPY, (b) NVDA, (c) MSFT, (d) LLY, (e) GS, and (f) AVGO, with 7, 7, 9, 11, 11, and 9 calendar days to expiry, respectively. Circles and diamonds distinguish reported call and put IV. Solid, dashed, and dotted curves show per-ticker neural, sector neural, and global parametric fits. The legend applies to all panels. The MSFT and LLY fits overpredict IV near the money (Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:smile_date_bias}).}}{10}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:per_ticker_panels}{{1}{10}{In-sample comparison of the implied volatility (IV) observed on May 11, 2026 with surfaces fitted across all fifteen calibration snapshots. Panels show (a) SPY, (b) NVDA, (c) MSFT, (d) LLY, (e) GS, and (f) AVGO, with 7, 7, 9, 11, 11, and 9 calendar days to expiry, respectively. Circles and diamonds distinguish reported call and put IV. Solid, dashed, and dotted curves show per-ticker neural, sector neural, and global parametric fits. The legend applies to all panels. The MSFT and LLY fits overpredict IV near the money (Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:smile_date_bias})}{figure.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces In-sample comparison of CRR American-option prices with Alpaca indicative mids across moneyness for SPY, NVDA, LLY, and GS on May 11, 2026. Errors are tree price minus indicative mid. Curves show prices from the parametric, sector, and per-ticker surfaces and, for reference, from the reported IV. The gray ribbon is the median local bid--ask half-spread by moneyness bin. A point inside the ribbon has an absolute pricing error below that local half-spread.}}{11}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:price_error}{{2}{11}{In-sample comparison of CRR American-option prices with Alpaca indicative mids across moneyness for SPY, NVDA, LLY, and GS on May 11, 2026. Errors are tree price minus indicative mid. Curves show prices from the parametric, sector, and per-ticker surfaces and, for reference, from the reported IV. The gray ribbon is the median local bid--ask half-spread by moneyness bin. A point inside the ribbon has an absolute pricing error below that local half-spread}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Temporal-holdout comparison. Training used six snapshots labeled April 14 through April 22, 2026, and testing used April 23--24. Every test ticker appeared in training. Errors and gaps are in volatility percentage points. Configuration B used different row sets from A and C.}}{12}{table.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:earnings_holdout}{{2}{12}{Temporal-holdout comparison. Training used six snapshots labeled April 14 through April 22, 2026, and testing used April 23--24. Every test ticker appeared in training. Errors and gaps are in volatility percentage points. Configuration B used different row sets from A and C}{table.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Simulated evolution of GS stock prices (a), put values (b), and call values (c) along 1{,}000 paths between April 28 and May 29, 2026. Time is measured in calendar days from April 28. Black curves show the median at each date, and shaded bands contain the middle 90\% of values. Red and blue curves show group medians for paths ranked in the 1st--5th and 95th--99th percentiles of terminal stock price. Option values are dollars per share and represent the cost of repurchasing the contract for a short seller. Entry premiums were \$16.51 per share for the put and \$16.09 for the call.}}{13}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gs_paths}{{3}{13}{Simulated evolution of GS stock prices (a), put values (b), and call values (c) along 1{,}000 paths between April 28 and May 29, 2026. Time is measured in calendar days from April 28. Black curves show the median at each date, and shaded bands contain the middle 90\% of values. Red and blue curves show group medians for paths ranked in the 1st--5th and 95th--99th percentiles of terminal stock price. Option values are dollars per share and represent the cost of repurchasing the contract for a short seller. Entry premiums were \$16.51 per share for the put and \$16.09 for the call}{figure.caption.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Simulated evolution of annualized IV at the GS put strike (a) and call strike (b). These IV paths come from the scenario in Fig.~\ref {fig:gs_paths}. Both panels express IV as percentages on the same vertical scale. Curves show pointwise medians across all 1{,}000 paths and within the terminal-stock groups defined in Fig.~\ref {fig:gs_paths}. Shading shows the 5th--95th percentile band. Time is measured in calendar days from April 28, 2026, and stops before expiration because the payoff at expiration does not depend on IV.}}{13}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gs_iv}{{4}{13}{Simulated evolution of annualized IV at the GS put strike (a) and call strike (b). These IV paths come from the scenario in Fig.~\ref {fig:gs_paths}. Both panels express IV as percentages on the same vertical scale. Curves show pointwise medians across all 1{,}000 paths and within the terminal-stock groups defined in Fig.~\ref {fig:gs_paths}. Shading shows the 5th--95th percentile band. Time is measured in calendar days from April 28, 2026, and stops before expiration because the payoff at expiration does not depend on IV}{figure.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Effect of linking IV shocks to stock returns on short-option P\&L before expiration, by stock return, after ten trading days for (a) GS and (b) LLY. Each difference is coupled-factor P\&L ($\rho =-0.6$) minus uncoupled-factor P\&L ($\rho =0$) on the same stock path, with the same independent normal shocks. Negative values indicate worse short-position P\&L under coupling. The 3{,}000 paths per ticker are sorted by stock return and divided into ten groups of 300. Symbols show each group's median P\&L difference at its median stock return, and lines connect the symbols. Shaded bands span each group's 25th--75th percentiles. These bands describe variation across paths, not confidence intervals. Red circles denote puts and navy diamonds denote calls. All prices are evaluated on May 12, 2026, with 17 calendar days to expiration.}}{14}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dynamic_ablation}{{5}{14}{Effect of linking IV shocks to stock returns on short-option P\&L before expiration, by stock return, after ten trading days for (a) GS and (b) LLY. Each difference is coupled-factor P\&L ($\rho =-0.6$) minus uncoupled-factor P\&L ($\rho =0$) on the same stock path, with the same independent normal shocks. Negative values indicate worse short-position P\&L under coupling. The 3{,}000 paths per ticker are sorted by stock return and divided into ten groups of 300. Symbols show each group's median P\&L difference at its median stock return, and lines connect the symbols. Shaded bands span each group's 25th--75th percentiles. These bands describe variation across paths, not confidence intervals. Red circles denote puts and navy diamonds denote calls. All prices are evaluated on May 12, 2026, with 17 calendar days to expiration}{figure.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Five-session option forecasts for the shorter-maturity cohort, selected with 10--21 calendar DTE at each origin. Scores average matched options within each ticker and origin before averaging dates. MAE scores the forecast mean. MAE, CRPS, and central 90\% interval width are in dollars per share, and coverage is date-weighted. Dates better counts the origins on which a variant's date-level MAE was below that of frozen IV. Every IV variant uses the same stock paths, origin IV, and matched quotes.}}{15}{table.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:forecast_main}{{3}{15}{Five-session option forecasts for the shorter-maturity cohort, selected with 10--21 calendar DTE at each origin. Scores average matched options within each ticker and origin before averaging dates. MAE scores the forecast mean. MAE, CRPS, and central 90\% interval width are in dollars per share, and coverage is date-weighted. Dates better counts the origins on which a variant's date-level MAE was below that of frozen IV. Every IV variant uses the same stock paths, origin IV, and matched quotes}{table.caption.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Five-session forecasts of GS stock prices (a), put values (b), and call values (c), compared with observations from August 4 to August 11, 2026. The put strike is \$1{,}000, the call strike is \$1{,}105, and both options expire on August 21. Forecasts use the July-cutoff surface and information available at the August 4 origin. Navy curves show forecast means from 1{,}000 stock paths, with the coupled factor used for option values. Shading spans the central 90\% of simulated values. Red dashed curves show frozen-IV mean option forecasts on the same stock paths. Black points show observed stock closes and option midpoints, with vertical bars spanning Alpaca's indicative bid and ask. Time is measured in trading sessions from the origin. This is the earliest GS origin with both selected shorter-maturity options observed at the five-session endpoint.}}{16}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:chronological_forecast}{{6}{16}{Five-session forecasts of GS stock prices (a), put values (b), and call values (c), compared with observations from August 4 to August 11, 2026. The put strike is \$1{,}000, the call strike is \$1{,}105, and both options expire on August 21. Forecasts use the July-cutoff surface and information available at the August 4 origin. Navy curves show forecast means from 1{,}000 stock paths, with the coupled factor used for option values. Shading spans the central 90\% of simulated values. Red dashed curves show frozen-IV mean option forecasts on the same stock paths. Black points show observed stock closes and option midpoints, with vertical bars spanning Alpaca's indicative bid and ask. Time is measured in trading sessions from the origin. This is the earliest GS origin with both selected shorter-maturity options observed at the five-session endpoint}{figure.caption.10}{}}
\citation{cont2001}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Five-session stock forecasts in 2025 and August--September 2026. Each ticker has 245 origins in 2025 and 17 in 2026. MAE scores the forecast median. MAE, CRPS, and central 90\% interval width are in dollars per share. Scores average dates within each of three 10{,}000-path simulation seeds and then average the seeds. Adaptive volatility denotes the Gaussian model with EWMA decay $0.97$ selected on historical data. Historical selection set the direction-predicting model's drift to zero, which made it identical to the adaptive-volatility model, so the table omits it. The unchanged-price benchmark has no uncertainty interval. The last column counts origins on which the adaptive-volatility model had lower date-level CRPS than JumpHMM.}}{17}{table.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:stock_validation}{{4}{17}{Five-session stock forecasts in 2025 and August--September 2026. Each ticker has 245 origins in 2025 and 17 in 2026. MAE scores the forecast median. MAE, CRPS, and central 90\% interval width are in dollars per share. Scores average dates within each of three 10{,}000-path simulation seeds and then average the seeds. Adaptive volatility denotes the Gaussian model with EWMA decay $0.97$ selected on historical data. Historical selection set the direction-predicting model's drift to zero, which made it identical to the adaptive-volatility model, so the table omits it. The unchanged-price benchmark has no uncertainty interval. The last column counts origins on which the adaptive-volatility model had lower date-level CRPS than JumpHMM}{table.caption.11}{}}
